  "final_message": "**vs_default=0.9675** \u2014 confirmed again (three measurements: 0.9667, 0.9667, 0.9675 \u2014 all tightly clustered, meas_spread\u22640.0010). The winning strategy is locked.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc1 Final Summary\n\n**Winning strategy:** `block_threads(n=1024)` \u2014 a single L3 instruction-level decision.\n\n| Configuration | latency_ms | vs_default | meas_spread |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Default (512 threads) | 0.1182 ms | 1.000 (baseline) | \u2014 |\n| block_threads=128 | 0.1628 ms | 1.371 \u274c | 0.0003 |\n| block_threads=256 | 0.1496 ms | 1.266 \u274c | 0.0018 |\n| **block_threads=1024** \u2705 | **0.1143 ms** | **0.9667\u20130.9675** | **0.0004\u20130.0010** |\n| block_threads=1024 + vectorize j\u00d74 | ~0.14 ms | unstable (noisy) | 0.099 |\n| block_threads=1024 + vectorize j\u00d78 | 0.1161 ms | 0.9781 | 0.0079 |\n| block_threads=1024 + unroll j\u00d74 | 0.1146 ms | 0.9678 | 0.0011 |\n\n**Why this works:** RMSNorm is a two-pass row-wise reduction over N=4096. Doubling the block size from the default 512\u21921024 threads means each thread handles 4 elements (vs. 8), giving a shallower warp-shuffle reduction tree (32 warps vs. 16). This reduces synchronization latency in the `__syncthreads` + warp-reduce steps that gate both passes, at minimal occupancy cost (still 34 blocks/SM across 30 SMs). The win is **decisive** \u2014 3.3% speedup confirmed reproducibly on 3 independent measurements with near-zero spread (\u22640.1%).",
